  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nA few showers and storms today, but mainly just a stray \nshower/storm. Satellite trends not showing a low inland with the sea \nbreeze today, but recent history makes it hard to say there's no \nchance.  Thus have a 20% chance along the seabreeze for the \nafternoon. Otherwise, the rest of the afternoon will feature hot \ntemps similar to the past several days. Heat advisory remains in \neffect through the afternoon and a few sites have reached advy \ncriteria as of early afternoon. \n\nOverall picture with high pressure in place remains. The ridge \ncenter is north of the area with a few short waves rotating east to \nwest under the ridge. For Tuesday into Wednesday moisture levels \nlook too low to warrant more than low PoPs (mainly Tuesday). \nMoisture levels will increase though Thursday - topping over 2\", \neven 2.25\" along the coast which is pushing PoPs up in some models, \nincluding the NBM.  Feel though that some guidance may be a bit too \nexcited, and have capped PoPs over land closer to 50%. Expect \nscattered showers and storms, but not a widespread rain out day. \nAlso, rainfall totals are not expected to be impressive. \nProbabilities of greater than 0.1\" are pretty good along the coast, \nbut when you look at greater than 0.5\", they are near zero. Even a \nlittle rain is better than none, though!\n\nHot temperatures are expected again on Tuesday, but deep moisture \ndecreases quite a bit and as a result of mixing, surface RH will \nalso decrease more efficiently than today, so we're only borderline \non a heat advisory tomorrow. It may be necessary, but confidence \nisn't there at the moment. As we head toward Wednesday and Thursday \nwe should see at least a bit of relief from the upper 90s and temps \nover 100, but unless showers/storms persist over an area on \nThursday, would still expect near to slightly above normal temps. \nLow temperatures will decrease a bit late in the period with drier \nair moving in. High temps return to above 100 west and in the mid \nand upper 90s east for the weekend.",
